Subject: Re: LING/POLI: Western Sci = Eastern Sci ?

There is this trend to see Western and Eastern views as different.
Then their is the mostly PC crowd who talks about African by way of
Eqypt influences on Greek (read: Western) culture/thought.

This was/is mostly taken jokingly, but a recent article in Science
News showed that in fact Neubin (sp?) culture had influenced the
culture/ thought/science of Eqypt.

I suspect that there has been far more mixing of ideas across cultures,
"races," etc. then we have been lead to believe.

So I say, "east = west;" that there is no real difference; west tends
in the last few hundred years to strongly weight direct observation,
statisical methodology, etc. The east seems to stress long term
observation of 100's or 1000's of years and a larger degree of fuzzy
logical (if you will). Some non western health care seems put more
stress on things like diet, and takes advantage of plecbo (sp?)
effects, etc.

Both eastern and western culture has rational and non-rational thoughts;
I accept the rational and question the non-rational. (I consider 500
years of evidence that treatment X "treats" problem Y to be reasonably
rational. And the thought, "Bill Cinton will solve the problems of our
country" to be rather non-rational.)

/Harry

