Date: Wed, 20 Jan 93 20:35:16 EST
From: dplatt@cellar.org
Subject: I&A: Hip PC/Call for calls

I have not received any extropian mail for two days and am beginning to go 
through withdrawal. I meant just to post a quick test message, but decided a 
little content couldn't hurt too much.

Over the last couple weeks there has been sporadic discussion of my project 
by Hal, Nick, Garret, Tim and probably a few others. I appreciate extropian 
intelligences applied to this subject. 

One area of discussion was the social acceptability of wearing your computer. 
This is a serious concern, but there are so many markets in which it doesn't 
matter, that I don't think it is a showstopper. This was basically Nick's 
conclusion. He listed military, medical, industrial and other applications as 
promising target markets - and these are indeed some of the markets I expect 
VARs to make a lot of money with. For the business person, I also agree with 
Nick that a notebook or subnotebook with chordic input might be a good 
product. What I envision is a subnotebook with a hybrid keyboard. This hybrid 
keyboard would consist of 31 keys with close to normal size, travel and 
placement. Touch typists would only have to learn chords for numbers, 
function keys, etc.... A to Z would be qwerty touch typable. But running 
TryChorder software, one handed chording would be available also. Velcro it 
to your vest, and tilt the screen, and it becomes a lower tech wearable 
leaving one hand completely free to hold briefcase, phone or whatever. All in 
a package about 4x6x1 inch....

Another way of dealing with the social acceptability issue is to use text to 
speech with a tiny earphone as the output device rather than the Private Eye. 
This would work fine for a business man in a meeting who wants to take notes, 
and reference spreadsheet data. 

Somehow or another we're going to have to deal with the social responses to 
wearing gear if we expect to start taking STAYING ALIVE more seriously. I 
just bought a bicycle helmet to wear while I drive for additional head 
protection in a crash, and to experiment with mounting the Private Eye. 

As to chording being something that will take some effort to become 
accepted....that's why I've had TryChorder written. Soon, you'll all be able 
to make your own conclusions about this form of input.

Garret wrote something about my company, Select Tech, Inc. becoming the hub 
of a wheel of interconnected extropian companies, while Tim (citing Perry) 
mentioned something to the effect that serious business is not going to be 
discussed in such a public forum. Well, just for the record, let me state 
that Select Tech already is the hub of a secret conspiracy of extropian 
companies that you don't know about because your not one of us insiders! Or 
did I dream that? All seriousness aside, I would like to work with others on 
this list, but it is a tricky thing. Being a fan of Harry Browne's HOW I 
FOUND FREEDOM IN AN UNFREE WORLD, I am always on my guard for what he calls 
"group traps". I prefer to work with people as individual subcontractors and 
would like to avoid ever having to have employees. Yet most potential 
investors want to see a business plan that assumes a traditional 
organizational structure. And the problem with attempting to only work with 
independent contracters is that negotiations become ongoing and complex. Any 
ideas on this? Similarly, the kinds of deals I favor with potential 
money-sellers (investors) are non-traditional and creative and possibly 
overly complex. I would like to keep the whole thing self-financing. We'll 
see.

Apparently, I'm not too paranoid about discussing much of my strategy on the 
net, but I do take Perry's and Tim's ideas here seriously. I recently aquired 
pgp 2.1 and will post my public key shortly. For those of you who want to buy 
Hip PCs, find out more about my plans, etc., I recommend investing in a phone 
call to 215-277-4264. Day or night...if I am not in you can leave a long 
voice mail message, and I will call you back. To all who have sent me private 
e-mail, TryChorder s/w and Hip PC info are still scheduled for end of this 
month distribution. Thanks for your patience.

AND NOW THE CONTEST!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!(only those who have read this far get to 
enter)

The year is 1995 and wearable computers with hmd and chordic input have taken 
off. 

Best 2000 word or less scenario highlighting the wonderfullness of this tech, 
gets the author a really great prize to be determined later. If its not a 
really great prize.....and/or it is determined way later, my reputation will 
suffer. You  retain all rights to what you write. I will ask for permission 
to use it as part of the TryChorder manual.

That's all for now. 

