Subject:  PHIL: 'Western' Rationalism

Oh, dear, we don't have any students of the Classics among us, do we?

"Western rationalism" is one of those redundancies that is used partly
because of force of habit and partly because it ensures a higher level of
communication than merely "rationalism."

"The West" and "rationalism" basically originated in the same time and
place, Ancient Greece.  This is where Reason was born (la naissance), and
where the idea of freedom was born, and the distinction between societies
which practiced both rationality and liberty from those that didn't also
originated.

It so happened that the main opponent of the ancient Greek rationalist
libertarians (if you will) was Persia, as chronicled by the first historian,
Herodotus, in "The Persian Wars."  Persia was towards the sunrise (a.k.a.
the Orient, the 
East) from Greece, hence the terming of this contrast geographically, i.e.,
East v. West.

Needless to say, this preceeded the Middle Ages, which preceeded the Renais-
sance (explicitly, the Rebirth of Reason upon the rediscovery of the
rationalist works of antiquity, Greece and Rome), which preceeded the
Englightenment.  Or this would be needless if some of my esteemed fellows
hadn't placed the origin of the tradition of western rationalism in the
Enlightenment, about 17 centuries late.  Not that this implies any fault
on their part; part of my place in this info-catallaxy is the economy of
scope of the history of ideas.  Max and Tom are also philosophy students,
but never do seem to take up its history in this forum.

I now return you to your regularly scheduled flame-war.

