As you recognize, it is not surprising in itself that some particular
vastly-improbable event has occurred -- if you shuffle a deck of
cards, you will get an arrangement, one of 8e67, which almost
certainly has never occurred before nor will again.  Your method of
dealing with this, the bit with the "number of things considered",
is not clear to me.  

To test the hypothesis that "synchronicitous" events occur with
unusual probability, you would have to specify all possible events
and sequences of events that could be called "synchronicitous" or
otherwise.  This could be difficult.  Then you'd need to fix a
subset of those which you would describe as "synchronicitous", and
see whether their occurrence is distinguishable from that expected.

As we know that people tend to notice things that they find
interesting, it seems a peculiar worldview to postulate a force
which can freely rearrange events, knows everyone's personality and
intellect, knows what they find odd -- and spends its time messing
with people's minds.

