Subject: Re:  PPL: Restitution is Usually Not Enough

> I own a road and I require you to get accident insurance before I'll 
> let you drive on it...what makes the driver insurance requirement
> acceptable is the fact that, in principle, the driver would have the
> option to post a bond instead of buying insurance...
 
> Robin Hanson
 
Wrong!  What makes the requirement acceptable is the fact that you own that
road.  You also have the right to require people to shave their heads before
allowing them to use your property.  They have the equal right to refuse; to
make alternate transportation arrangements.
 
You have proposed that criminals pay not only the cost of the crime for
which they were apprehended and convicted (including logically related
expenses), but also the costs of other, unrelated crimes.  I say that if
there was no prior agreement to follow this procedure it is unlibertarian.  
 
Is the driver insurance requirement (an unforced agreement between motorist
and road owner prior to use of the road) the same as theft insurance with no
prior agreement?  If you force someone to pay for something he didn't do (or
agree in advance to pay), the way I see it, YOU are the agressor.
 
I agree with your point that disproportionate response is necessary to deter
crime.  A "heads I win (don't get caught), tails I break even (get caught
and make restitution)," would seem to invite crime.  What I'm saying is you
can't violate Libertarian principle to accomplish this.  If you do, you
might as well call yourself a statist, because this is exactly how States
act.  
 
The whole point of being a State (rather than a private business) is to have
an unequal relationship with it's citizens (customers); one that can't be
reversed.  If you and I have an equal relationship, what I do to you, you
can do to me.  What the State does to us, in contrast, we can't do to the
State.  
 
In a free society, you can legitimately force enough restitution from a
criminal to balance the scale--you're doing to him what he did to you; the
relationship is equal (reversible).  If you can get away with taking MORE
than that, however, the relationship is unequal.  You've acted like a State.
 
There are ways to accomplish the goal of disproportionate response without
initiating physical coercion.  Tom Morrow wrote an excellent post on that
subject.  If people know that you are a criminal, they have the option of
causing you additional loss by withdrawing cooperation from you.  (For
example, about six months ago a nationwide organization was formed which
allows landlords to check on prospective tenents; to determine whether they
have ever cheated another landlord.  Prior to this, statewide agencies were
the largest available.)  Libertarianism allows this, because no force is
being initiated.
 
Another approach is my idea is to build disproportionate response into the
agreement allowing you to live in a particular private community.  (This
affects everyone--not merely thieves.  Anyone could be framed for a crime he
didn't commit.)  If the community required, upon conviction, restitution
plus punitive damages 1000 times greater than that, I wouldn't agree to live
there--it's too risky.  On the other hand, if the punitive damages were
twice those of restitution, I  withdraw my objection.  After all, I'm not
only honest, I'm cautious and alert.  In the extremely unlikely event I'm
busted for a theft I didn't commit, I could afford to just pay the penalty.
 
If we ever accept an unequal relationship, we are on the slippery slope of
Statism.  The only difference between us and totalitarians would be a matter
of degree. 
 
Of course, I seriously doubt that a totalitarian could tell the difference
between me and Robin.  :-)
 
