Date: Thu, 21 Jan 1993 06:02:20 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: PPL: Insurance for Criminals

In his excellent essay, Robin Hanson states that "a criminal cannot
cheaply insure against the risk of being caught, due to moral hazard
considerations."  The moral hazard cited is the disincentive of the
criminal to avoid being caught and/or convicted, if he is insured
against same.  Similarly, selling restitution insurance to a victim
before a crime is committed takes away incentive for customers 
to adequately protecting themselves.  On the other hand, a victim can 
buy insurance after a specific crime without creating a moral hazard, 
because it is up to the enforcer to catch the criminal and extract the
restitution.  However, there is still some moral hazard here; the 
victim could buy restitution insurance and hire a cheap, ineffective 
enforcer. 

But moral hazards exist in almost any kind of insurance, and are
dealt with by establishing risk profiles of the customer and by
prescribing rules as part of the contract.  For example, driving
records are used to set insurance rates.  Thus, an insured driver 
retains an incentive to lower future insurance rates by avoiding
accidents and following those rules of the road considered important
by insurers.  There is often a large deductible, which retains some 
incentive on the part of the customer to lower the risk.

Similarly, a post-crime restitution insurer can base his rates
on the effectiveness of the enforcement agency hired by the victim.  
A pre-crime restitution insurer can base his rates on the safety of 
the neighborhood, the effectiveness of the customer's crime-prevention
agency, alarm system, etc.  There can be a large deductable.  The 
insured has incentive to increase safety both by following the
rules and preventing loss of the deductable.

Now let's reconsider insurance for the criminal.  For post-crime
insurance, the insurer can require that the criminal hire an
anti-enforcement agency, whose job is to protect the criminal from
enforcement agencies.  This service might involve hiding, ID change,
physical defense, rescues, etc. depending on the fee and
circumstances.  In addition, a pre-crime criminal insurer could give 
big discounts for training in criminal techniques and partnership
with skilled criminal agencies. 

The customer might get a big discount if he can establish a history of 
committing crimes without getting caught, an illegal-credit rating.
In this case, committing crimes enhances reputation, while getting 
caught lowers it.  The criminal might have to prove he committed 
these crimes in "anti-court".  The illegal-credit rating/anti-court 
service must be accessible to the crime insurers but kept hidden 
from enforcers; this requires very leak-proof pseudonyms.

Skilled, near-zero-risk, profitable crime seems to emerge.  In response, 
enforcement and restitution insurance costs, and therefore punishment
costs, must increase.  In the face of escalating punishments, a 
criminal who values his life and/or freedom very highly could buy 
severe-penalty insurance of various kinds:

* Rescue insurance, in which up to X lives and $Y will be spent
  by the enforcement agency to spring the criminal from prison;
* Eye-for-eye insurance, in which the insurer is contracted to
  extract a punishment from the enforcer greater than or
  equal to punishment of the insured -- eg, if the insured
  is executed, the insurer is obligated to kill at least
  one of the enforcer's personnel.  Ten-eyes-for-an-eye insurance
  would cost more, but would it cost ten times more?

What emerges may be open warfare.

On a brighter note, the current state does not use economics to 
set punishment, and indeed the (punishment)*(risk of conviction) seems 
to be less than the benefit for certain kinds of "crimes".
This suggests that a criminal insurance system could be profitably 
operated to protect certain "criminals" from the state, assuming
this anti-system could keep itself sufficiently hidden and secure.
In this way statist expropriations, regulations, etc. might be ignored
with minimal risk where benefits >> transaction costs.

